In Java, when a subclass overrides a method from its superclass,
 it can return a more specific type (a subtype) than the one 
 declared in the superclass method. This is called a covariant return type

- It allows more precise return types while still respecting polymorphism.
- Improves type safety and reduces the need for casting.
- Makes overridden methods more context-aware in subclasses.
